1
00:00:24,263 --> 00:00:29,811
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:30,952 --> 00:00:39,104
We are going to continue our discussion on VR Navigation Experience Optimization, which we started earlier.

3
00:00:40,326 --> 00:00:51,942
So, earlier we talked about one way to address the problem of minimizing the possibility of CS or cyber sickness.

4
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:56,296
in a system-controlled virtual tour.

5
00:00:56,316 --> 00:00:57,278
And how did we do that?

6
00:00:57,678 --> 00:01:16,540
We made use of one approach where we tried to figure out an optimal path covering all vertices in a given graph representing the virtual world which can be traversed in minimum time.

7
00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:19,684
That was one approach.

8
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:28,681
There is one more approach which we are going to talk about in this lecture.

9
00:01:28,701 --> 00:01:41,057
Now, that other approach is essentially to find out an optimal path covering the maximum number of points of interest or POIs given some time constraints.

10
00:01:41,077 --> 00:01:42,919
So, earlier we did not have a time constraint.

11
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,684
Our objective was to find a path covering all vertices.

12
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:48,912
that can be traversed in minimum time.

13
00:01:49,012 --> 00:01:59,005
Now we are imposing one time constraint and our objective is to find out a path, given this time constraint, where the number of POIs covered will be maximum.

14
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:10,038
So, in order to find that time constraint, time threshold, we need to define such a threshold, a time threshold.

15
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:12,942
So, earlier we saw how to compute an optimal tour.

16
00:02:13,951 --> 00:02:17,675
where the path covers all nodes in minimum time.

17
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:33,670
The problem is that that minimum time may still be more than the time typically associated with the onset of cyber sickness.

18
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:43,519
So, that minimum time, although it is minimum given all possible paths, may still be responsible for inducing cyber sickness.

19
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:50,154
It may still be more than the time threshold above which CS occurs.

20
00:02:50,194 --> 00:02:54,720
So, we are talking about something called a time threshold.

21
00:02:54,820 --> 00:03:06,195
So, essentially, our objective should be to restrict the movement time, or the experience time of the user, or the VR exposure time of the user, within this threshold.

22
00:03:06,515 --> 00:03:09,399
If we cross it, then there is a possibility of cyber sickness.

23
00:03:12,262 --> 00:03:15,126
Now, let us call this time threshold TTH.

24
00:03:15,798 --> 00:03:16,599
as shown here.

25
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:30,013
So, essentially, what we are trying to do is find an optimal path that completes within the threshold time.

26
00:03:31,875 --> 00:03:39,063
Now, when I say complete, essentially that means that the user should be able to cover the optimal path within this time threshold.

27
00:03:41,405 --> 00:03:44,809
And this time threshold can be the duration of the

28
00:03:46,392 --> 00:03:49,816
virtual tour, the maximum duration of the virtual tour.

29
00:03:54,281 --> 00:03:57,625
Now, of course, there is no easy way to find out the time threshold.

30
00:03:57,745 --> 00:04:06,395
Of course, there are some studies which recommend some thresholds, which depend on the content.

31
00:04:10,199 --> 00:04:16,146
Sometimes it may also depend on the speed of movement and so on.

32
00:04:16,784 --> 00:04:25,633
And we can obtain the threshold value through some empirical study with a limited number of users.

33
00:04:26,995 --> 00:04:28,837
That is one possibility.

34
00:04:28,857 --> 00:04:34,663
We can also use some pre-existing study results to decide on a threshold.

35
00:04:37,526 --> 00:04:46,335
Now, when we are talking of a time threshold and when we are talking of covering a tour or completing a tour within the given time threshold,

36
00:04:49,015 --> 00:05:04,572
it is not mandatory and not always possible also to visit every single node or vertex which represents the points of interest.

37
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:18,887
So, essentially, here what we are saying is that we want the user to complete a tour within a given time threshold and during that tour, we are fully aware of the fact that

38
00:05:19,238 --> 00:05:24,445
the user may not be able to cover all points of interest, unlike in the previous approach.

39
00:05:29,493 --> 00:05:40,268
Now, in order to take this into account to formulate our overall problem, we defined a term called the importance

40
00:05:42,104 --> 00:05:45,028
of every node, how important a node is.

41
00:05:45,529 --> 00:05:49,995
Unless we know the importance, we do not know which nodes to cover within the given time threshold.

42
00:05:53,840 --> 00:06:09,681
In order to do that, we can define an importance function, a function that computes importance or assigns importance to every node, which is in the form f, v maps to the set of positive real numbers.

43
00:06:11,956 --> 00:06:28,126
In other words, every vertex is mapped to every vertex or every point of interest, V belonging to the set of V, maps to a positive real number which indicates the importance.

44
00:06:28,146 --> 00:06:31,392
So, we are capturing the importance in terms of a positive real number.

45
00:06:32,113 --> 00:06:34,858
The more the number is, the higher the importance.

46
00:06:38,281 --> 00:06:44,447
Then, once we define the idea of importance for every node, what can we do?

47
00:06:46,109 --> 00:06:49,773
We can compute the total importance of a given path.

48
00:06:49,873 --> 00:06:56,680
Suppose we have a path P containing the nodes V1 to Vn.

49
00:06:58,302 --> 00:07:01,525
Now, each node has its importance.

50
00:07:02,887 --> 00:07:06,010
So, the total importance of the path can be defined as

51
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:16,274
fp, which is a summation of fvi, where i varies from 1 to n, and n is the number of nodes in the path.

52
00:07:20,881 --> 00:07:32,318
It is a quite obvious formulation that to identify or determine total importance, we simply add up the individual importance of each node in a given path.

53
00:07:35,302 --> 00:07:49,617
So, then, in terms of that, we can have a problem statement: we want to maximize Fp, that is, the total importance for paths that can be completed within the time threshold TH.

54
00:07:50,218 --> 00:07:59,267
In other words, we want to basically identify a path which

55
00:08:00,682 --> 00:08:13,599
takes time less than or equal to the threshold time, but in which we are maximizing the total importance.

56
00:08:13,619 --> 00:08:19,928
Now, there is another important aspect that needs to be considered, which is the waiting time.

57
00:08:19,988 --> 00:08:29,541
So, at a given point of interest, the user is expected to spend some time to inspect or to

58
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:40,375
do something with that point of interest, as we have indicated in our earlier lectures during the experiments we have done.

59
00:08:43,078 --> 00:08:50,489
So, this waiting time is also a concern when we are trying to figure out a path maximizing the total importance.

60
00:08:53,273 --> 00:08:58,060
Now, this waiting time is essentially the time required by the user to inspect or interact

61
00:08:58,495 --> 00:09:04,423
with a particular object at a node that is the point of interest.

62
00:09:08,168 --> 00:09:12,614
So, like the importance function, we can also have a waiting time function.

63
00:09:13,215 --> 00:09:24,330
So, we can define this function, the waiting time function, as W where V is mapped to a real positive number.

64
00:09:24,431 --> 00:09:27,675
In other words, every node is mapped to a real positive number.

65
00:09:28,735 --> 00:09:49,858
which indicates the maximum time required to inspect or interact with the object that is there at V. So, this is the maximum time a user requires to spend at the point of interest.

66
00:09:50,919 --> 00:09:56,565
So, now we have two quantities: one is importance,

67
00:09:57,811 --> 00:09:59,172
and the other one is waiting time.

68
00:09:59,273 --> 00:10:06,160
So, two quantities are associated with each node: one is importance, and the other one is the waiting time.

69
00:10:08,983 --> 00:10:13,328
Like total importance, we can also calculate total waiting time in a similar manner.

70
00:10:13,368 --> 00:10:26,822
Suppose we have a path P having all these vertices v1 to vn, n nodes, each node has an associated waiting time; then the total waiting time w

71
00:10:27,781 --> 00:10:41,857
capital W capital P equals the summation of individual waiting times for all the nodes i equal to 1 to n. So, again, it is quite obvious, like the total importance calculated earlier.

72
00:10:45,101 --> 00:10:50,487
Then we need to revise our earlier problem statement.

73
00:10:50,507 --> 00:10:51,409
What is that revision?

74
00:10:52,069 --> 00:10:56,995
We want to maximize the total importance

75
00:10:57,768 --> 00:11:04,439
for paths that complete within the time threshold TH, taking into account the waiting time.

76
00:11:10,548 --> 00:11:25,632
So, essentially what we want to state is TP plus WP must be less than or equal to T threshold, where we are also maximizing FP.

77
00:11:29,932 --> 00:11:31,374
Now, how do we solve this?

78
00:11:31,554 --> 00:11:34,798
This is a well-known problem in operations research.

79
00:11:35,479 --> 00:11:43,429
It is called a generalization of a well-known problem, that is, the vehicle routing problem or VRP.

80
00:11:46,273 --> 00:11:49,377
This is called VRP with profit, or VRPP.

81
00:11:49,997 --> 00:11:56,125
For more details on this problem, you can refer to the material cited at the

82
00:11:57,691 --> 00:12:00,495
end of this lecture.

83
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:13,451
So, the idea here is that we do not need to visit all the nodes; instead, we want to maximize profit within the given time constraints.

84
00:12:13,511 --> 00:12:18,958
So, here the profit is in terms of the importance of the nodes.

85
00:12:19,579 --> 00:12:24,525
So, we want to maximize the importance within the given time constraint without

86
00:12:25,197 --> 00:12:29,241
necessarily requiring us to visit all the nodes.

87
00:12:29,262 --> 00:12:38,568
Like in the previous approach where we talked about TRP solvers, so existing tools available to solve the traveling salesman problem,

88
00:12:38,568 --> 00:12:50,445
here also we can make use of existing VRPP solvers, so vehicle routing problem with profit solvers.

89
00:12:51,117 --> 00:12:59,667
to get the optimum path that maximizes profit within the given time constraint, that is, the threshold time.

90
00:13:01,509 --> 00:13:06,034
And maximizing profit essentially maximizes importance, the total importance.

91
00:13:09,078 --> 00:13:20,331
So, in other words, we want to get a path that lets the user visit the maximum number of the most important POIs.

92
00:13:20,935 --> 00:13:30,528
This is a rephrasing of the overall problem that we are trying to solve.

93
00:13:30,548 --> 00:13:35,855
And if you look at this statement, you can see that this will serve our purpose: given a threshold,

94
00:13:35,855 --> 00:13:49,052
a time threshold beyond which cybersickness may occur, we want to restrict the user's exposure below that threshold time and

95
00:13:49,505 --> 00:13:57,275
within that time, we want to ensure that the user is able to visit the most important POIs, as many as possible.

96
00:13:57,295 --> 00:14:06,066
So essentially, we are trying to figure out a path that will achieve these objectives.

97
00:14:09,590 --> 00:14:11,673
So let us quickly go through the algorithm.

98
00:14:12,073 --> 00:14:15,698
So the input is the graph.

99
00:14:16,235 --> 00:14:29,914
Remember that during our earlier discussion, we talked about converting the virtual environment to a graph, which is not necessarily a complete graph, but a fully connected graph.

100
00:14:29,934 --> 00:14:45,195
So, we have this original graph G having the vertex set V and edge set E epsilon, along with the importance f and waiting time w specified for

101
00:14:45,597 --> 00:15:01,176
each of the nodes, and the output that we want is this optimal path P where F is maximized and the total traveling time plus wait time is less than the threshold.

102
00:15:01,917 --> 00:15:07,544
So, earlier we talked about only traveling time, but now we also have to take into account the wait time.

103
00:15:07,564 --> 00:15:15,013
So, traveling time plus wait time, which together represent the total exposure time, must be less than the time threshold.

104
00:15:18,824 --> 00:15:30,497
So, the first thing is, as before, what we need to do is compute the complete graph G dash, where we will be replacing the edges that are not

105
00:15:30,497 --> 00:15:37,985
directly connected with the shortest path between those vertices.

106
00:15:39,307 --> 00:15:40,908
This is what we did before also.

107
00:15:40,948 --> 00:15:42,630
So, the same thing we will be doing here.

108
00:15:43,771 --> 00:15:46,114
Convert the graph to a complete graph.

109
00:15:48,592 --> 00:15:58,422
Then again, the second stage is also the same: replace the edge weights, which are originally the Euclidean distance between the two nodes in the

110
00:15:58,422 --> 00:16:10,957
modified graph, with the travel time t as we did before, where travel time we compute using the natural walking model.

111
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:17,704
Then, once this is given,

112
00:16:18,578 --> 00:16:23,704
Also, we have separately specified the importance and the wait time.

113
00:16:24,424 --> 00:16:47,269
So, we then need to use the VRPP solver with input the final graph, that is G double dash, which contains vertices and edges where the edges are weighted with the duration of travel between the

114
00:16:48,397 --> 00:16:51,560
vertices which define the edges.

115
00:16:55,864 --> 00:17:04,963
So, essentially, we will be using a solver on this modified graph G double dash, and also we will be providing the input values for f, the waiting

116
00:17:04,963 --> 00:17:12,219
the importance of each node, and w, that is the waiting time for each node, to get the optimal path P opt.

117
00:17:15,983 --> 00:17:18,285
So, here we will be using the solvers

118
00:17:18,805 --> 00:17:29,742
solve VRPP where the input is G double dash, F, and W, then we get the output as P opt.

119
00:17:29,762 --> 00:17:37,775
Now, like before, what we need to do is basically modify the output to reflect the original graph.

120
00:17:40,099 --> 00:17:45,167
So, what we need to do is there will be edges that were not originally connected in the

121
00:17:45,738 --> 00:17:53,268
Virtual graph virtual world representation, but we introduced hypothetical edges in the form of shortest paths.

122
00:17:53,408 --> 00:18:00,577
So, we need to replace those edges to reflect the original nature of the graph.

123
00:18:00,597 --> 00:18:06,265
So, replace hypothetical edges joining VI, VJ with the shortest path VI, VJ.

124
00:18:11,692 --> 00:18:13,534
Then we need to return

125
00:18:14,830 --> 00:18:22,639
the optimal path which is modified after replacing the edges with the shortest path.

126
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:32,407
So, this is the algorithm: we are first creating the graph, then converting it to a complete graph, then

127
00:18:32,407 --> 00:18:39,437
converting it to a graph with edge weights equal to the travel duration, where travel is the

128
00:18:39,923 --> 00:18:46,751
duration computed using the natural walking model to give a realistic experience.

129
00:18:48,153 --> 00:18:57,989
Then this graph, along with the importance and wait time at each node, is provided as input to the VRPP solver, and then

130
00:18:57,989 --> 00:19:09,238
the output is further modified to replace the hypothetical edges with the shortest path to finally get the optimum path.

131
00:19:09,826 --> 00:19:26,080
that can be covered with the maximum number of most important POIs within the given time threshold, taking into account waiting time at each node.

132
00:19:26,100 --> 00:19:28,602
So, let us summarize what we have learned so far.

133
00:19:30,544 --> 00:19:33,927
So, we are talking about experience optimization.

134
00:19:34,207 --> 00:19:39,732
So, there we talked about a couple of approaches to take care of the CS issues.

135
00:19:40,994 --> 00:19:53,001
cyber sickness issues. First, we talked about identifying an optimal path in a given graph representation of a virtual environment which can be covered in

136
00:19:53,001 --> 00:20:03,850
The minimum time now for a given large environment, this minimum time may not be addressing the cyber sickness concern because this

137
00:20:03,850 --> 00:20:08,925
time may still be large enough to induce cyber sickness. So, in order to address that,

138
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:21,672
we are going for another optimal path formulation where we talked about node weights, where we talked about the node importance and waiting times.

139
00:20:22,613 --> 00:20:30,213
Then we talked about a time threshold, and then we are trying to find out an optimal path that can be covered by the

140
00:20:30,213 --> 00:20:37,427
user within the time threshold, taking into account the importance of each node and the waiting time at each node.

141
00:20:38,538 --> 00:20:48,006
In other words, this optimal path will allow the user to cover the maximum number of the most important POIs within a given time threshold,

142
00:20:48,006 --> 00:20:57,677
taking into account the time required to spend at each point of interest to inspect or interact with the object present there.

143
00:21:01,606 --> 00:21:09,535
So, in the next part of the lecture, we will have a quick look at how this idea has been put into practice in the context of our case study.

144
00:21:11,297 --> 00:21:18,806
So, looking forward to meeting you again in the next part of this lecture, which will be the final part of this particular topic of experience optimization.

145
00:21:20,107 --> 00:21:22,930
So, I hope you could follow the discussion.

146
00:21:24,372 --> 00:21:26,955
Thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.
